WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:16.430
THE STATE OF GAME DEVELOPERS:
Usually, software engineers are some of the

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:19.310
best paid employees in the entire world.

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:24.880
They have great job prospects and some of
them are even able to earn 7 figures.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:30.040
Now, of course, there are people on the other
side of the spectrum as well who are freelancing

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:32.500
and barely making ends meet.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:37.980
But, the people who are really in a sticky
situation are the ones that are in the middle.

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:43.460
These guys generally work for extremely well
known brands like EA, Activision Blizzard,

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:48.370
and Ubisoft, but they’re often treated like
Amazon warehouse workers.

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:53.530
This is quite surprising given that software
developers are white collar workers and are

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:56.579
essential in bringing these games to market.

00:00:56.579 --> 00:01:01.500
Not to mention, the amount of games that these
companies release per year has reduced substantially.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:06.430
So, you would think that software engineers
would have less on their plate, but this is

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:07.430
not the case.

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:13.610
In fact, software engineers routinely work
12 hours per day, six days a week.

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:19.240
And when a game is getting close to its launch
day, this ramps up to 13 hours per day, seven

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:20.570
days per week.

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:23.320
This isn’t some sort of new discovery either.

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:28.320
This has been common practice within the gaming
industry dating as far back as 2004.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:34.090
In fact, EA has already settled two class
action lawsuits regarding labor abuse, but

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:39.760
20 years later, nothing much has changed at
EA, and this is the same case with Activitision

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:43.229
Blizzard, Ubisoft, and even Epic Games.

00:01:43.229 --> 00:01:48.270
Given how demanding these companies are, you
would think that they atleast pay well.

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:50.770
That must be why people are sticking around
right?

00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:53.659
Well, that’s not true either.

00:01:53.659 --> 00:01:57.880
While these companies do definitely pay a
livable wage, it’s nowhere close to what

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:01.990
these software engineers could be earning
if they spent their efforts elsewhere.

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:03.820
Take EA for example.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:11.920
Associate engineers earn $71,000 per year
while senior engineers earn $171,000 per year.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:17.570
This sounds good until you realize that Google
pays their entry level engineers $180,000

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:21.700
per year and their senior engineers $326,000
per year.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:26.540
So, here’s how these game developers trap
engineers and get away with working them to

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:29.879
the bone while paying them below market wages.

00:02:29.879 --> 00:02:35.840
THE DANGERS OF PASSION:
You commonly hear the career advice of follow

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:40.450
your passion and that if you do something
you love, you’ll never work a day of your

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:41.450
life.

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:46.550
But, this advice completely falls apart when
you factor in companies that abuse their employees’

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:47.550
passion.

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:52.240
The gaming industry is just one example of
this, but this issue is present throughout

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:53.890
the entire job market.

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:58.090
The companies with the best working conditions
are generally those with products that people

00:02:58.090 --> 00:02:59.860
aren’t exactly passionate about.

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:05.450
I mean, I don’t think anybody is particularly
passionate about writing algorithms that collect

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:07.010
other people’s data.

00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:11.890
So, companies like Facebook and Google have
to pay larger salaries and offer better perks

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:16.470
to get people to jump through some moral hoops
and do work that doesn’t exactly excite

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:17.470
them.

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:22.280
On the flipside, companies like Tesla and
SpaceX are able to pay far lower salaries

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:26.980
and demand much more because the people who
work there are extremely passionate about

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:29.550
the final product and company mission.

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:33.080
Game companies very much fall on this side
of the spectrum.

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:38.530
The software engineers that apply to these
companies are generally avid gamers themselves.

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:43.380
And they’re usually super motivated to bring
games that gave them so much joy to other

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:44.530
players.

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:48.310
This sentiment usually gets them trapped in
a vicious loop.

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:52.950
As one game developer put it, “it tends
to be more fun at the beginning of a project

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:55.560
and a lot of drudgery at the end.”

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:59.189
Just when you think that you might want to
switch jobs, you’re given a new project

00:03:59.189 --> 00:04:02.230
and your passion for the art is reignited.

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:08.150
And before you know it, you keep going down
the same rabbit hole over and over and over.

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:13.900
One EA executive has even admitted that toxic
environments are an inevitability within large

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:14.970
publishers.

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:19.500
And the worst part is that the class action
lawsuits that we talked about arguably just

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:21.780
made things worse for these developers.

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:26.639
For example, the second lawsuit against EA
resulted in many employees being reclassified

00:04:26.639 --> 00:04:29.820
as hourly workers instead of salaried workers.

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:34.250
The reasoning behind this was that hourly
workers would have to be paid overtime while

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:36.520
salaried workers did not.

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:41.660
This seems like a good thing until you realize
that hourly workers are ineligible for stock

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:45.271
based compensation which is where the money
is really at.

00:04:45.271 --> 00:04:50.370
At Google, for example, well over half of
principal engineers’ compensation is in

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:51.620
the form of stock.

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:54.300
And that’s not even accounting for stock
growth.

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:59.080
We all know that the FAANG companies have
killed it over the past 10 years, but some

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:02.780
of these game developers have actually grown
even more than FAANG companies.

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:07.750
EA, for example has grown 10X in the past
10 years while Activision Blizzard has grown

00:05:07.750 --> 00:05:08.880
5X.

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:14.100
And much of this growth is attributable to
the work being done by game developers, yet

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:18.880
many of them wont even benefit from the company
doing so well given that they’re treated

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:20.120
as hourly workers.

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:25.430
So, unfortunately, it doesn’t seem like
following their passion has actually worked

00:05:25.430 --> 00:05:28.650
out all that well.

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:33.310
TRAPPED:
While many developers get stuck in this vicious

00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:39.060
loop for their entire careers, some do try
to look for other opportunities but it doesn’t

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:42.050
take them long to realize that they’re kind
of stuck.

00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:47.780
Here’s the thing, while EA and Google both
need software engineers, the work that engineers

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:50.600
do at these companies couldn’t be more different.

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:55.020
At EA, software engineers are primarily going
to be working to develop things like collision

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:59.330
detection, collision response, physics, animations,
and user inputs.

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:04.810
Meanwhile, at Google, software engineers are
primarily going to be working on search algorithms,

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:09.419
sorting through data, debugging production
issues, and identifying edge cases.

00:06:09.419 --> 00:06:14.650
Virtually none of this has to do with programming
great physics or fluid game mechanics.

00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:19.490
And while most programmers agree that game
development is way harder, companies don’t

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:24.990
hire employees based on how hard their previous
work was, companies hire based on how relevant

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:27.349
and applicable the previous work was.

00:06:27.349 --> 00:06:32.460
So, it’s typically quite difficult for engineers
that spent their entire careers in game development

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:34.780
to switch to traditional software engineering.

00:06:34.780 --> 00:06:37.930
This issue is only made worse with time.

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:42.060
As software engineers progress through the
ranks, their jobs have less to do with coding

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:46.479
and more to do with systems design and leveraging
enterprise solutions.

00:06:46.479 --> 00:06:51.629
This includes things like Google Cloud Platform,
Microsoft Azure, Amazon Web Services, Oracle

00:06:51.629 --> 00:06:54.289
apps, SAP, Salesforce, and so on.

00:06:54.289 --> 00:06:58.580
With that being said, when a game developer
with 10 years experience interviews for a

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:02.900
software engineer position, the questions
are going to be less about reversing linked

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:08.270
lists and creating binary trees and more about
how familiar they are with SAP S4/Hana or

00:07:08.270 --> 00:07:09.639
AWS.

00:07:09.639 --> 00:07:13.530
The developers that are competing against
the game developer are likely to have a traditional

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:17.210
background and will thus be able to answer
these questions much better.

00:07:17.210 --> 00:07:21.840
Now, you might be asking, why not just apply
for a lower level position.

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:27.139
Well, the problem is that it’s even harder
to get these jobs because you come off as

00:07:27.139 --> 00:07:29.020
being overqualified.

00:07:29.020 --> 00:07:33.060
In an ideal world, recruiters would take a
closer look at the game developers background

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:37.250
and let them interview for the more junior
role so that they can learn the ropes on the

00:07:37.250 --> 00:07:38.400
tech side of things.

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:43.830
But, more times than not, recruiters dismiss
such applicants as engineers with a lack of

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:44.830
trajectory.

00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:48.889
If a given developer has 10 years of experience
but they still aren’t familiar with systems

00:07:48.889 --> 00:07:54.430
design or enterprise solutions, the applicant
comes off as someone who isn’t growing and

00:07:54.430 --> 00:07:58.850
learning, and no one wants to hire someone
that isn’t growing and learning.

00:07:58.850 --> 00:08:04.590
So, while some game developers try venturing
out, they’re often hit with a bunch of rejection

00:08:04.590 --> 00:08:09.800
and they end up staying at their current role
or switching to yet another game company,

00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:13.210
and the cycle just continues.

00:08:13.210 --> 00:08:18.919
DISCRIMINATION:
It’s not all terrible news though as there

00:08:18.919 --> 00:08:22.419
are a few game companies prioritize employee’s
mental health.

00:08:22.419 --> 00:08:27.430
One of these companies is Riot Games who is
the developer behind League of Legends and

00:08:27.430 --> 00:08:28.430
Valorant.

00:08:28.430 --> 00:08:32.649
Not only does Riot Games rank favorably when
it comes to workplace culture, they actually

00:08:32.649 --> 00:08:34.029
rank at the top.

00:08:34.029 --> 00:08:40.060
In fact, 95% of Riot employees say that it’s
a great place to work while the average for

00:08:40.060 --> 00:08:42.300
a US company is only 57%.

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:47.279
But, even Riot falls victim to one of the
biggest isues within the game development

00:08:47.279 --> 00:08:50.820
industry which is gender based discrimnation.

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:56.610
Just a few years ago, Riot’s entire leadership
was men and they actually got sued for discrimiation.

00:08:56.610 --> 00:08:59.450
They ended up settling for $100 million.

00:08:59.450 --> 00:09:04.601
Since then, Riot has diversified their leadership
to include 20% women, but it doesn’t seem

00:09:04.601 --> 00:09:07.540
like the sentiment has changed all the much.

00:09:07.540 --> 00:09:12.470
Yes, Riot has expanded their diversity, but
it appears that this is more of a show to

00:09:12.470 --> 00:09:14.970
appease courts than a true shift in culture.

00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:18.240
A similar situation can be found at Ubisoft
as well.

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:24.370
In fact, an internal survey at Ubisoft revealed
that 25% of employees had seen or experienced

00:09:24.370 --> 00:09:25.820
workplace misconduct.

00:09:25.820 --> 00:09:30.720
In addition to this, 1 in 5 said that they
didn’t feel fully respected or safe in the

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:31.720
work environment.

00:09:31.720 --> 00:09:36.130
Since then, Ubisoft has gone ahead and fired
a bucnho their executive team.

00:09:36.130 --> 00:09:40.811
But to be honest, there’s not much that
these companies can do because that’s not

00:09:40.811 --> 00:09:42.529
where the issue really stems from.

00:09:42.529 --> 00:09:48.760
The issue really stems from the gaming community
itself which can oftentimes be quite toxic.

00:09:48.760 --> 00:09:51.850
This isn’t just limited to gaming per se.

00:09:51.850 --> 00:09:56.310
Basically any online community where you can
mask your identity is pretty toxic.

00:09:56.310 --> 00:10:00.820
And given that the gaming community is likely
the largest community in which this is possible,

00:10:00.820 --> 00:10:05.010
it’s no wonder that they’re also arguably
the most toxic.

00:10:05.010 --> 00:10:08.860
Of course, this isn’t to say that all gamers
are toxic.

00:10:08.860 --> 00:10:14.580
The vast majority of perfectly normal people,
but it just takes a few bad actors here and

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:16.390
there to gain a bad reputation.

00:10:16.390 --> 00:10:21.220
And if you want an example of just how toxic
online communities can get, I recommend checking

00:10:21.220 --> 00:10:23.420
out the YouTube channel: MaskedArab.

00:10:23.420 --> 00:10:28.950
The YouTuber basically goes onto Omegle dressed
as an Arab and basically everyone is racist

00:10:28.950 --> 00:10:29.950
to him.

00:10:29.950 --> 00:10:34.680
It’s actually pretty funny because the YouTuber
generally roasts these people pretty hard,

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:37.510
but it’s also sad that people can be so
toxic.

00:10:37.510 --> 00:10:41.589
Anyway, when you combine this online toxicity
with the fact that hardcore gaming communities

00:10:41.589 --> 00:10:46.639
are dominated by men, it’s not surprising
that women working at these game companies

00:10:46.639 --> 00:10:50.610
often have to deal with a bunch of discrimination
and toxicity.

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:56.519
THE VERDICT:
In the end, most game companies are nostalgic

00:10:56.519 --> 00:11:02.380
to many of us as we grew up seeing their loading
animations over and over again, and many of

00:11:02.380 --> 00:11:06.500
us have sunk more hours into these games than
we’d like to admit.

00:11:06.500 --> 00:11:10.420
Considering this, it’s not surprising that
many of these people would like to enter game

00:11:10.420 --> 00:11:15.570
development themselves and bring joy to a
new generation of people, but unfortunately,

00:11:15.570 --> 00:11:17.829
the industry is not all that great.

00:11:17.829 --> 00:11:21.880
First of all, given that you're driven by
passion, it’s easy for these mega companies

00:11:21.880 --> 00:11:26.020
to take advantage of that and demand more
work while paying relatively less.

00:11:26.020 --> 00:11:30.190
And if you get far into a game development
career, it’s often hard to switch to a traditional

00:11:30.190 --> 00:11:34.520
software engineering role as you often don’t
possess the knowledge and expertise that would

00:11:34.520 --> 00:11:36.440
be expected of someone at your level.

00:11:36.440 --> 00:11:40.760
And if you’re a woman, all of these issues
are just going to be amplified even more.

00:11:40.760 --> 00:11:43.450
So, what’s the solution to all of this?

00:11:43.450 --> 00:11:47.040
Well, theoretically speaking, it’s actually
really simple.

00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:51.480
Instead of only following passion, the key
would be to follow a combination of passion

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:52.899
and opportunity.

00:11:52.899 --> 00:11:57.170
If you’re a programmer interested in game
development, the answer might be to join Twitch

00:11:57.170 --> 00:11:58.490
or Discord.

00:11:58.490 --> 00:12:02.529
These companies deal with much more traditional
engineering skills like integration with AWS.

00:12:02.529 --> 00:12:07.649
So, you’d be able to participate in and
grow the gaming community while not being

00:12:07.649 --> 00:12:09.829
subject to its various pitfalls.

00:12:09.829 --> 00:12:13.820
And if enough people start thinking like this
and move away from traditional development

00:12:13.820 --> 00:12:19.670
companies, then maybe the situation may just
get better, but that’s just what I think.

00:12:19.670 --> 00:12:21.650
Would you want to be a game developer?

00:12:21.650 --> 00:12:23.010
Comment that down below.

00:12:23.010 --> 00:12:27.329
Also, drop a like if you hope that conditions
at these gaming companies get better.

00:12:27.329 --> 00:12:31.070
And of course, consider checking out our discord
community to suggest future video ideas and

00:12:31.070 --> 00:12:36.880
consider subscribing to see more questions
logically answered.

